I would humbly suggest that this effort has better chances of success if
it is done at the moment when the `DC' math fonts become available,
since otherwise even sites that only use the LaTeX format will still
have to keep the CM fonts around for the greek mathematic characters. If
you suggest a move now, then all the North American TeX administrators
will revolt :-))
 
Seriously, changes that make existing dvi-files `rubbish' should not be
made too often. You could `sell' all of the DC fonts to people who only
write in English by showing them how much better the DC *math* fonts are
than the original CM ones, and by stating that there will not be another
font change for some time.
 
At the time the DC math fonts are made available you could perhaps also
consider abandoning the magstep scheme for font sizes, since DC fonts
can be generated at `any' size. Savings in disk space, which I believe
also was one of the original reasons for the magsteps, no longer happen
since in a typical TeX installation the generated pk-bitmaps end up in
different parts of the directory tree depending on the MF mode (e.g.
/usr/local/lib/texmf/fonts/tmp/pk/cx/ and
/usr/local/lib/texmf/fonts/tmp/pk/ljfour/).
 
Maybe you could then even make @xipt 11 points (instead of 10.95),
@xivpt 14 points (instead of 14.4) and so on ... :-)
 
